# host-screen-contracts Demo Deep Dive

Generated on 2026-05-02 from local analysis of `host-screen-contracts`, local CLI runs, generated input/output artifacts, tests, and web research.

## Executive Thesis

`host-screen-contracts` is most valuable when positioned as a deterministic evidence layer between legacy host-screen transactions and API/wrapper modernization work. It is not a wrapper, emulator, migration platform, or code generator. Its defensible core is narrower and stronger: record a host-screen path, turn it into a reviewable transaction contract, produce an OpenAPI candidate, create replay evidence, detect drift, produce privacy artifacts, and benchmark package readiness before teams invest in wrapper implementation.

That matters commercially because legacy modernization programs often fail or overrun when teams begin implementation before they understand the transaction shape, field semantics, error paths, drift risk, privacy constraints, and replayability. The repo creates a concrete artifact set that lets modernization sponsors, API owners, host SMEs, security teams, QA teams, and partner delivery teams make evidence-based decisions earlier.

The most credible ROI is not "automatic modernization." The credible ROI is reduced discovery time, fewer wrapper rework cycles, earlier privacy approval, drift detection before outages, better modernization backlog prioritization, safer partner handoff, and higher confidence that the first wrapper implementation is aimed at the right transaction contract.

## External Market Context

The web research supports the repo's business premise:

- The U.S. GAO reported in July 2025 that the federal government spends over $100 billion annually on IT, that agencies typically report about 80 percent of IT spending on operations and maintenance, and that legacy systems can create cost and cybersecurity challenges. GAO also found that incomplete modernization plans increase the likelihood of cost overruns, schedule delays, and project failure. Source: https://www.gao.gov/products/gao-25-107795
- The OpenAPI Specification defines a standard, language-agnostic interface that helps humans and computers understand API capabilities without source code or network traffic inspection. That aligns with this repo's OpenAPI output as a review artifact rather than deployed server code. Source: https://spec.openapis.org/oas/v3.1.1.html
- IBM's Integrated Web Services for IBM i page frames APIs and web services as a path for externalizing and reusing existing ILE business logic. That supports the business story that teams often want to expose existing host logic without rewriting it first. Source: https://www.ibm.com/support/pages/integrated-web-services-ibm-i-web-services-made-easy
- IBM's 5250 ENPTUI documentation shows that 5250 full-screen applications can include richer constructs such as push buttons, check boxes, radio buttons, subfile selection lists, windows, menu bars, and pull-down menus. That supports the repo's attention to screen structure, subfiles, fields, function keys, layout, and drift rather than treating traces as simple text. Source: https://www.ibm.com/docs/en/hats/9.7.0?topic=poct-enabling-enhanced-non-programmable-terminal-user-interface-enptui
- NIST's Privacy Framework describes privacy risk as arising from system, product, and service operations with data across the data lifecycle, with organizational impacts such as noncompliance costs, revenue loss, brand harm, and cultural harm. That supports the repo's privacy-report, sanitize-trace, tokenization, redaction, and local-only design. Source: https://www.nist.gov/privacy-framework/getting-started-0
- NIST SP 800-53 Rev. 5 provides a security and privacy control catalog intended to manage risk from threats, errors, structural failures, and privacy risks. This supports treating host-screen trace handling as a controlled evidence workflow. Source: https://csrc.nist.gov/Pubs/sp/800/53/r5/upd1/Final

